NAME
    uniq - collapse adjacent duplicate lines

SYNOPSIS
    uniq [-c] [-D] [-w <chars>]

DESCRIPTION
    Collapses ADJACENT duplicate lines only, matching real uniq's own
    semantics - it is NOT a global dedup, so pipe through 'sort' first if
    that's what's actually wanted ('regs | sort | uniq'). -c prefixes each
    remaining line with its consecutive repeat count.

    -D prints every line of each run of two or more adjacent duplicates,
    and nothing of a line that has none: the duplicates themselves, not
    one of each. -c and -D together are refused, as GNU uniq refuses them.

    -w <chars> (also -w<chars>) compares only the first <chars>
    characters of each line, so lines that differ after them count as
    duplicates; the first line of a run is the one kept.

EXAMPLES
    watch log 1 | sort | uniq -c
    rom hash -r --comparable / | sort | uniq -D -w 40
